reduced in abun\u0002dance by 10%\u000430% in total lysates of DKJT cells compared\nL808@P8: to KHis cells and the single chaperone deletions (Figure 6C). Inter\u0002estingly, two small ribosomal proteins (RpsQ S17 and RpsT S20)\nL809@P8: were increased in abundance, an effect that was also detected in\nL810@P8: DKJ cells (Figure 6C).\nL811@P8: In summary, the combined loss of DnaK/DnaJ and TF results\nL812@P8: in a pronounced proteostasis collapse characterized by the\nL813@P8: aggregation of large, multidomain proteins, disruption of proper\nL814@P8: protein flux through GroEL/ES and defective ribosomal biogen\u0002esis. These findings explain the strong growth defect of DKJT\nL815@P8: cells at 30\u0003C and their inability to grow at higher temperatures.\nL816@P8: DISCUSSION\nL817@P8: The DnaK Interactome\nL818@P8: The DnaK interactome characterized here comprises at least\nL819@P8: \u0001700 proteins in WT cells and \u00011,000 proteins in TF-deleted\nL820@P8: cells, demonstrating the pervasive role of the Hsp70 chaperone\nL821@P8: system in protein folding and proteostasis. While the vast\nL822@P8: A B\nL823@P8: C\nL824@P8: (672)\nL825@P8: DnaK\u0002interactors\nL826@P8: (357)\nL827@P8: Additional\nL828@P8: DnaK-interactors\nL829@P8: in ΔT/K cells\nL830@P8: 553 119 90 39 318\nL831@P8: (248)\nL832@P8: GroEL\nL841@P8: GroEL substrates\nL842@P8: GroEL substrates\nL843@P8: class I class II class III\nL844@P8: 20 2618\nL845@P8: GroEL substrates\nL846@P8: increased on DnaK\nL847@P8: upon GroEL depletion\nL848@P8: GroEL substrates\nL849@P8: decreased in cells\nL850@P8: upon GroEL depletion\nL851@P8: (38) (44)\nL852@P8: Identifications (%)\nL853@P8: D\nL854@P8: Class I Class II Class III\nL855@P8: 0\nL856@P8: 20\nL857@P8: 40\nL858@P8: 60\nL859@P8: 70\nL860@P8: 50\nL861@P8: 30\nL862@P8: 10\nL863@P8: Degraded\nL864@P8: Aggregated\nL865@P8: His\nL866@P8: Figure 5. GroEL Substrates Accumulate on DnaK in GroEL-Depleted Cells\nL972@P9: KHis\nL973@P9: ΔKJ\nL974@P9: GroEL-depl